Q: SET spring.jackson.serialization.write-dates-as-timestamps &amp; date-format to ISO1806 globally A: <pre><code>@Bean
@Primary
public ObjectMapper objectMapper(Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder builder) {
    ObjectMapper objectMapper = builder.build();
    objectMapper.configure(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS, false);
    return objectMapper;
}
</code></pre>

Q: How Framer.com&#39;s editor/main window and canvas/preview of website passes data internally? A: <p>I checked framer's editor, it was doing it in an amazing way. It created new messageChannel first, then transferred that new channel's port using normal postMessage. That's it, the handshake is done, now it can communicate using that channel.</p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/iVSAc24j.png" alt="onRendezvousMessage(e, n) { if (!this. shouldHandleRendezvous(e, n)) return; let t = new MessageChannel; if (d(e-ports " title="Framer website's messageChannel code." /></p>
<p>Architecture:<br />
1. Create a new MessageChannel.<br />
2. Handshake by transferring the port of that channel.<br />
3. Done! transfer data using that message port.</p>
<p><strong>In main window:</strong></p>
<pre><code>const channel = new MessageChannel();

iframe.contentWindow.postMessage(
  { type: &quot;connect&quot; },
  iframeOrigin,
  [channel.port2]
);

// From now on Continue with channel.port1:
channel.port1.postMessage();
</code></pre>
<p><strong>In iframe:</strong></p>
<pre><code>window.addEventListener(&quot;message&quot;, (event) =&gt; {
  const port = event.ports[0];
  port.onmessage = (e) =&gt; {
    // get data by: e.data;
  };
});
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Benefits of cross-origin and using custom channel:</strong><br />
security, isolation, and framer can add plugins from the community, and don't hack websites of other people who are building their website using framer. (my opinion): Cause if it was same origin plugin script, a cloud easily edits the content of that website and inserts malicious code.  And for a custom channel, it will not need to call message events over and over. It makes it easy and isolated communication.</p>
<p><strong>My recommendation</strong> ( not 100% sure worked for me) : Use this if you are communicating with iframe or building complex app like framer.</p>
<p><strong>Bellow there is a real example run and get the idea:-</strong></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const channel = new MessageChannel();
const iframe = document.getElementById("iframe");

iframe.onload = () =&gt; {
  iframe.contentWindow.postMessage({
      type: "connect"
    },
    '*',
    [channel.port2]
  );
};

// Continue with channel.port1:
window.addEventListener('mousemove', (e) =&gt; {
  channel.port1.postMessage({
    x: e.clientX,
    y: e.clientY
  });
})</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>body {
  background-color: skyblue;

}

iframe {
  background-color: white;
  margin-top: 10px;
  width: 160px;
  height: 150px;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>Sky blue part is main window. Move mouse to see.
&lt;br&gt;
&lt;iframe id="iframe" srcdoc="
  &lt;b&gt;This is iframe!&lt;/b&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;mouse position &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;p id='msg'&gt;move your mouse in main window or click on main window &lt;/p&gt;

  &lt;script&gt;
    const msgEle = document.getElementById('msg');

    const initMsgEventHandler = (event) =&gt; {
      const port = event.ports[0];
      window.addEventListener('message', initMsgEventHandler);
      port.onmessage = (e) =&gt; {
        msgEle.innerText = `x: ${e.data.x} , y: ${e.data.y}`;
      };
    }

    window.addEventListener('message', initMsgEventHandler);
  &lt;/script&gt;

"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;</code></pre>
